\description{
The figure template function of Kaplan-Meier curves: one call makes the
whole figure of a \code{survfit2()} fit -- the curves, the censor marks, the
confidence band, the median and landmark lines, the number at risk
table below, the legend (with N and the median), the p-value and the
hazard ratio, the theme -- and returns it built once (a patchwork of
the curves and the table when there is a table).  It is the figure
content of the template \code{km_simple} (\code{figure: fig_km}).
}
\details{
The arguments are of two kinds.  What a statistician decides a figure
is one argument each (\code{type}, \code{ci}, \code{risktable}, \code{legend}, \code{pvalue} ...).
The look and the wording of a part is that part's list
(\code{censor_options = list(shape = "plus", size = 3)}): a name given
changes that one, the others keep their defaults.  A look left \code{NULL}
takes the figure style standard's value (\code{\link[=tfl_fig_style]{tfl_fig_style()}}: the study's
\verb{figure_style:} when tflspec writes the call, else the option
\code{tflspec.fig_style}, else tflspec's own).  What the arguments cannot
say is written with ggsurvfit's plot functions themselves (a code SPEC):
\code{+} after \code{fig_km()} does not reach the curves.

The numbers come from the fit (the medians, the number at risk, the
unstratified log-rank p-value) and from the ARD (the N of the legend,
\code{BIGN}; the hazard ratio, \code{HR}; a stratified p-value, \code{LOGRANK}).  No
model is fitted here.
}
